# Metal organic framework (ZIF-67)-derived Co nanoparticles/N-doped carbon nanotubes composites for electrochemical detecting of tert-butyl hydroquinone

Abstract In this work, the framework of Co nanoparticles and nitrogen-doped carbon nanotubes (Co/NCNTs) derived from metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) through solid pyrolysis was successfully synthesized in the atmosphere of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and Ar gases. The Co/NCNTs composites exhibited superior electrocatalytic performance for tert-butyl hydroquinone (TBHQ) oxidation due to the synergistic effect between chemical composition and the specific structure of Co nanoparticles and nitrogen-doped carbon nanotubes. It was found that the reaction underwent a quasi-reversible redox reaction with 37-fold higher anodic current for TBHQ at Co/NCNT-modified electrode when compared to bare glassy carbon electrode (GCE). In optimal condition, Co/NCNTs/GCE exhibited a linear range of $0.05{-}80.00~\mathrm{\upmumol.L^{-1}}$ for detecting TBHQ, and the limit of detection (LOD) was estimated to be as low as $10\ \mathrm{nmol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ (signal/noise of $S/N=3$ ). Moreover, the asobtained modified electrode was successfully used to assay the content of TBHQ in edible oil samples.
Keywords Nitrogen-doped carbon nanotubes (NCNTs); ZIF-67; Tert-butyl hydroquinone; Modified electrode
## 1 Introduction
The phenolic antioxidants play a significant role in preventing the decomposition of organic compounds in the food and pharmaceutical industries [1]. As one of the synthetic phenolic antioxidants (SPAs), tert-butyl hydroquinone (TBHQ) is widely used in edible oils and meat products, ascribed to its antioxidant ability, low cost and stability [2, 3]. But the excessive use of TBHQ is responsible for vision disturbance, convulsions and medullary dysfunction as reported by the national institute of health [4]. European Union (EU) Directive 95/2/EC formulates a maximum limit of $1~\mathrm{mg{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ in foods [5]. In China, United States, Australia, Brazil, New Zealand and Philippines, it has been permitted to use in foods up to a maximum limit of $200~\mathrm{{mg}{\cdot k g}^{-1}}$ [6]. Thus, it is necessary to develop an easy method for the determination of TBHQ to ensure the human health as well as fulfillment of legal requirements within the food industry.
Spectrophotometry [7], gas chromatography [8, 9], high performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) [10], capillary electrophoresis [11, 12], batch injection analysis [13] techniques have been established for the detection of TBHQ. Although these techniques are highly sensitive and selective to determine TBHQ, they have intrinsic shortcomings such as high cost and sophisticated sample preparation. In comparison with the methods above, electrochemical technique has inherent advantages of less expensive, time saving, and excellent sensitivity and selectivity. There are some reports for the electrochemical determination of TBHQ [14–25]. However, a major obstacle for the determination of TBHQ by bare solid electrode is the low current signal. An effective way to resolve this problem is modifying electrode by some advanced materials. Some nanomaterial-modified electrodes such as carbon materials [14–17], metal nanoparticles [18], metal oxides [19–21] and polymer composites [22–25] have been devised and used for the sensitive determination of TBHQ.
Among them, carbon materials, especially carbon nanotubes (CNTs) with high surface area and excellent conductivity, have received increasing attention as a promising electrochemical catalysis candidate. The introduction of heteroatoms (N, S, B) into CNTs could enhance the electrocatalytic properties. In particular, nitrogen doping has proved to be a promising method because nitrogen-doped carbon nanotubes (NCNTs) have excellent electron transport properties [26]. Traditional synthesized methods such as chemical vapor deposition (CVD) processes, arc-discharge or arc-vaporization strategies have disadvantages of harsh reaction conditions and difficulty of controlling the amount of N [27, 28]. At present, the approach of the solid-phase precursor carbonization (such as fullerene soot, organometallic compounds, polymers, metal–organic frameworks, etc.) [29–32] have been performed to synthesize CNTs due to their simpler equipment and more control over the final product. Metal organic frameworks (MOFs), comprised of metal clusters and organic linkers by coordinative bonding, play an important role in a variety of potential applications, especially in catalysis, gaseous fuel storage and chemical hydrogen storage [33, 34]. MOFs are considered as appropriate templates or precursors for the preparation of nanoporous carbon materials due to their unique porous structure and high specific surface area [35, 36]. The periodic arrangement of different atoms in MOFs provides congenital conditions for homogeneous atomic distribution. Therefore, the derived carbon-based composites possess uniform microstructure and well-dispersed metal nanoparticles to provide good electrochemical performances [37]. Specially, zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) as a subclass of MOFs are suitable for solid precursors with the existence of abundant carbon, nitrogen and catalytic active sites [38–40]. ZIF-67 contains abundant active cobalt sites [41]. Direct pyrolysis of ZIF-67 can produce cobalt incorporated N-doped carbon nanotubes composites as a bifunctional oxygen electrocatalyst [42].
In this paper, we report a fabrication of Co/NCNTs composites via the direct pyrolysis of ZIF-67 in an $\mathrm{Ar/H}_{2}$ atmosphere as illustrated in Scheme 1. Co/NCNTs composites were dropped on the bare glassy carbon electrode (GCE) and applied to the investigation of the electrochemical performance of TBHQ. As far as we know, there are few reports on the modification of GCE with $\mathrm{Co}/$ NCNTs composites to detect TBHQ. The Co/NCNTs composites modified electrode combined the advantages of the electronic conductivity and high surface area of NCNTs as well as more active sites of Co nanoparticles, which exhibited highly sensitive determination of TBHQ in oil samples. The sensor-based Co/NCNTs composites show good stability and acceptable selectivity.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Reagents
$\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ , 2-methylimidazole, tert-butyl hydroquinone (TBHQ), hydroquinone, butylated hydroxyanisole (BHA), glucose, propyl gallate (PG), butylated hydroxytoluene (BHT), N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) and other chemicals were purchased from Aladdin Industrial Co. Ltd., China. All the reagents were at least analytical grade and used with no further processing.
## 2.2 Apparatus
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were carried out on a Hitachi SU8020 microscope (Japan) operating at $3.0\mathrm{kV}$ . Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was performed with a JEOL JEM-2010 high-resolution TEM equipped with energy-dispersive X-ray detector (EDX). Powder X-ray diffractometer (XRD, Bruker D8-Advance) was used to verify the phase composition of samples. Electrochemical studies were measured on a CHI 660E electrochemical workstation (CH Instruments, China) with a conventional electrode system consisting of the bare or modified GCE (diameter of $3~\mathrm{mm}$ ) as work electrode, a platinum wire (CH Instruments, China) as a counter electrode and a saturated calomel electrode (SCE, Leici, China) as a reference electrode. HPLC analysis was measured by an Agilent 1260 chromatograph set at $280~\mathrm{nm}$ , equipped with an ultraviolet–visible (UV–Vis) spectrophotometer (SPD-10AV).
## 2.3 Synthesis of Co/NCNTs
ZIF-67 was prepared according to our previous report [43]. 2-Methylimidazole $(1.97~\mathrm{g})$ was dissolved in methanol 1 $\mathrm{40~mL}$ ). Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $(1.75~\mathrm{g})$ was dissolved in methanol $(40~\mathrm{ml})$ ). The 2-methylimidazole solution was quickly poured into the $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ solution under vigorous stirring for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ at the ambient temperature. The obtained purple precipitate was collected by centrifuging (8000 $\mathbf{r}{\cdot}\mathbf{m}\mathbf{\dot{i}}\mathbf{n}^{-1}$ , $30~\mathrm{min}\rangle$ and washed with ethanol three times and then dried in oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{~h~}$ to obtain ZIF-67 sample.
The ZIF-67 was pre-pyrolyzed at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $1.5\mathrm{h}$ under an $\mathrm{Ar/H}_{2}$ flow ( $95~\mathrm{vol}\%/5~\mathrm{vol}\%)$ with the ramp rate of $2\mathrm{\^{\circ}C}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . Then the temperature was increased to $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and maintained for $3.5\mathrm{~h~}$ to obtain Co/NCNTs product.
## 2.4 Fabrication of modified electrode
The GCE (diameter of $3.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ ) was polished with 0.30- and $0.05\mathrm{-}\upmu\mathrm{m}$ alumina slurry in turn to clear away the impurities and washed ultrasonically with ethanol and deionized water for $1~\mathrm{min}$ . $2~\mathrm{mg}$ Co/NCNTs composites were dispersed into $2~\mathrm{ml}$ DMF and sonicated for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ to obtain a homogeneous dispersion. Thereafter, ${8\upmu1}$ dispersed solution was dropped onto the bare GCE surface and dried at room temperature for electrochemical studies. The fabricated electrode was denoted as Co/NCNTs/GCE. ZIF-67/GCE was also prepared for comparison.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Characterization of Co/NCNTs composites
The morphology of the ZIF-67 and Co/NCNTs composites was observed by SEM. As shown in Fig. 1a, b, SEM images demonstrated that Co/NCNTs inherited morphology of the ZIF-67 crystals precursor which had the dodecahedron shape with the average size of $\sim900\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Compared with that of ZIF-67, the smooth surface of the Co/NCNTs composites was rough and numerous interlaced carbon nanotubes entwined on the surface of framework. Moreover, the interior and microscopic structure of the $\mathrm{Co}/$ NCNTs composites was further observed by TEM, as shown in Fig. 1c. The polyhedrons not only preserved the regular morphology of ZIF-67 but also formed the structure of N doped CNTs on the edge. Many dark dots of cobalt nanoparticles with different sizes were dispersed in the matrix. Particularly, Fig. 1d shows a partially enlarged view of the composite shell, and conformed NCNTs were distributed on the edge of the composites. Furthermore, a high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image revealed that the lattice fringes with an interplanar distance of $\sim0.34\ \mathrm{nm}$ corresponded to the C (002) plane and the $0.20\ \mathrm{nm}$ interplanar distance can be ascribed to the (111) plane of $\mathbf{Co}$ nanoparticles (Fig. 1e). Energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping was employed to illustrate the component analysis of Co/NCNTs composites, which are shown in Fig. 1f, suggesting that Co, N and C were distributed on the surface of Co/NCNTs composites uniformly.
Corresponding XRD patterns (Fig. 2) corroborated the successful fabrication of Co/NCNTs. As for ZIF-67, all diffraction peaks were consistent with the simulated ZIF67, demonstrating the successful synthesis of ZIF-67 crystal. As for Co/NCNTs, the characteristic diffraction peaks corresponding to (111), (200) and (220) planes were at $44.2^{\circ}$ , $51.5^{\circ}$ and $75.8^{\circ}$ , which matched well with a cubic body-centered structure of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ standard pattern (JCPDS Card no. 15–0806). A weak broad diffraction peak at $26^{\circ}$ was indexed as carbon nanotubes. The above results indicated the complete transformation from ZIF-67 to $\mathrm{Co}/$ NCNTs.
## 3.2 Electrochemical characterization of Co/NCNTs modified electrode
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) spectra can be applied to analyze the interfacial properties of the different electrodes (bare GCE, ZIF-67/GCE and Co/NCNTs/ GCE) in $0.1\ \mathrm{mol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ KCl containing $5.0\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]}^{3-/4-}$ as a redox probe. The interfacial charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ was equal to the diameter of the semicircle in the Nyquist plots of high frequency region. As depicted in Fig. 3a, the $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value of bare GCE was estimated to be $400~\Omega$ . When ZIF-67 was introduced on GCE, its $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value significantly exceeded that of bare GCE, confirming the poor electron conductivity of ZIF-67 materials. In sharp contrast, the Nyquist plot of Co/NCNTs/ GCE was an almost straight line due to the enhanced conductivity and fast electron transfer. The improved electrochemical characteristic implied that Co/NCNTs composites could be a promising candidate for sensing applications.
The electrode effective area was obtained by chronocoulometry. Figure 3b shows the chronocoulometry curves of different electrodes using $1\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}L^{-1}}\ \mathrm{K}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]$ solution in $1.0\ \mathrm{mol{\cdot}L^{-1}\ K C l}$ , and the effective surface area could be calculated by the Anson formula [44]:
$$
Q=2n F A c D^{1/2}t^{1/2}/\pi^{1/2}+Q_{\mathrm{dl}}+Q_{\mathrm{ads}}
$$
where $A$ refers to the electrode effective area, $F$ is the Faraday constant $(F=96500~\mathrm{C{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}$ ), $t$ is the time, $c$ is the substrate concentration, $n$ is the number of electrons transfer (n = 1), D is the diffusion coefficient (7.6 9 10-6 $\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ , $Q_{\mathrm{dl}}$ is the double-layer charge, and $Q_{\mathrm{ads}}$ is the Faradaic charge. The plots of $Q-t^{1/2}$ are shown in the inset of Fig. 3b. The slopes of the three lines were 7.09, 16.19 and 31.07 at the bare GCE, ZIF-67/GCE and Co/NCNTs/ GCE, respectively, and the calculated effective surface areas were 0.024, 0.055 and $0.106~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ for bare GCE, ZIF67/GCE and Co/NCNTs/GCE, respectively, indicating that the Co/NCNTs composites can significantly enlarge effective surface area.
## 3.3 Electrochemical response of TBHQ at Co/NCNTs/ GCE
The electrochemical behavior of TBHQ at different modified electrodes (bare GCE, ZIF-67/GCE and Co/NCNTs/ GCE) was demonstrated by cyclic voltammetry (CV) and differential pulse voltammetry (DPV). Figure 4a represents the CVs acquired for TBHQ $(0.10\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}L^{-1}})$ in $0.1\ \mathrm{mol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ phosphate buffer $\mathrm{(pH~7.0)}$ . It was found that the bare GCE gave a pair of redox peaks, which were located at $0.259{\mathrm{~V~}}$ $(E_{\mathrm{Ox}})$ and $-0.222\mathrm{~V~}$ $(E_{\mathrm{Red}})$ with the potential separation $(\Delta E_{\mathrm{p}})$ of $481~\mathrm{mV}$ . The oxidation and reduction peak currents of TBHQ were only 1.13 and $-1.61~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ , respectively. At the ZIF-67/GCE, the oxidation and reduction peak potentials of HQ were 0 and $-0.065\mathrm{~V~}$ , respectively. The $\Delta E_{\mathrm{p}}$ value of TBHQ at this electrode was reduced to $65~\mathrm{mV}$ , and the oxidation and reduction peak currents were about 5.03 and $-6.72~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ , respectively, which were higher than those of bare GCE. In contrast, $\mathrm{Co}/$ NCNTs/GCE revealed a pair of significant redox peaks, which were 0.014 and $-0.046\mathrm{~V~}$ , and $\Delta E_{\mathrm{{p}}}$ value was $60~\mathrm{mV}$ . In addition, the ratio of the anodic peak current to the cathodic peak current was almost equal to unity $(I_{\mathrm{pa}}$ $\langle I_{\mathrm{pc}}\ \approx\ 1{:}1\rangle$ , indicating the reaction of TBHQ at $\mathrm{Co}/$ NCNTs/GCE underwent a quasi-reversible redox reaction. The anodic peak current of the Co/NCNTs/GCE $(I_{\mathrm{pa}}=$ $42.24~\upmu\mathrm{A})$ was nearly 37 times and 6 times higher than that of bare GCE and ZIF-67/GCE, suggesting that $\mathrm{Co}/$ NCNTs/GCE exhibited better electrocatalytic activity to TBHQ compared to other electrodes.
Figure 4b shows DPVs of $0.1\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ TBHQ at different electrodes in phosphate buffer $(0.1\ \mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , $\mathrm{\pH=}$ 7.0). The oxidation currents of TBHQ at bare GCE, ZIF67/GCE and Co/NCNTs/GCE were detected to be $\sim4.64$ , 11.7 and $129.1~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ , respectively, indicating that the $\mathrm{Co}/$ NCNTs composites played a key role in promoting the electron transfer between analytes and the electrode. From the above results, the detection of TBHQ can be gained at Co/NCNTs composites modified GCE. The remarkable performance may be ascribed to their unique structural and compositional features. Co nanoparticles encapsulated by carbon shells is beneficial for accelerating electron transfer due to the electronic interaction between metal nanoparticles and CNTs [45–47]. Furthermore, NCNTs with high surface area and good conductivity are advantageous to enhance the mass transport of electrochemical reaction [48], improving electrochemical performance of TBHQ.
## 3.4 Electrochemical parameters of TBHQ at Co/ NCNTs/GCE
To investigate the electrochemical redox mechanism of TBHQ at Co/NCNTs/GCE, the CVs of $0.1\ \mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ TBHQ at various scan rates were obtained in Fig. 5a. The corresponding linear relationships of redox peak currents and square root of scan rate were displayed in Fig. 5b. As seen, both the anodic and cathodic peak currents of TBHQ increased linearly with square root of scan rate in the range of 20 to $300\ \mathrm{mV\cdots^{-1}}$ at Co/NCNTs/GCE with the equations as: Ipa = 324.4v –32.29 (R2 = 0.999) and Ipc = –364.1v1/2 $+31.49$ $(R^{2}=0.999)$ , respectively, demonstrating that the redox reaction of TBHQ at the as-obtained electrode was typical diffusion-controlled process.
In addition, the $\Delta E_{\mathrm{{p}}}$ value of the redox peak enlarged with the increase of scan rate, implying the quasi-reversible process of TBHQ at $\mathrm{Co/NCNTs/GCE}$ . Similarly, the linear relationship between peak potential and lgv was displayed ranging from 80 to $300\ \mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ in Fig. 5c and the equations were $E_{\mathrm{pa}}=0.0761\mathrm{g}\nu+0.123$ $\left(R^{2}=0.991\right)$ ) and $E_{\mathrm{pc}}=-0.0701\mathrm{g}\nu-0.162$ $(R^{2}=0.990)$ ), respectively. So, the electron transfer rate constant $(k_{\mathrm{s}})$ can be calculated according to the Laviron’s equation [49],
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{E_{\mathrm{{pa}}}=E^{\vartheta}+\displaystyle\frac{2.3R T}{(1-\alpha)n F}\lg\nu}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{E_{\mathrm{{pc}}}=E^{\vartheta^{\prime}}-\displaystyle\frac{2.3R T}{\gamma n F}\lg\nu}}\\ {{\lg{k_{s}}=\alpha\lg(1-\alpha)+(1-\alpha)\lg\alpha-1\displaystyle\mathbf{g}\frac{R T}{n F\nu}}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{\qquad-\alpha(1-\alpha)\displaystyle\frac{n F\Delta E_{\mathrm{p}}}{2.303R T}}}\end{array}
$$
wherein $E^{0^{\prime}},n,\ \alpha,\ \nu,\ k_{\mathrm{s}},\ R,\ T,\ F$ and $\Delta E_{\mathrm{p}}$ represent the formal potential, transferred electron number, electron transfer coefficient, scan rate, apparent heterogeneous electron transfer rate constant, molar gas constant, thermodynamic temperature, Faraday’s constant and difference of $E_{\mathrm{{pa}}}$ and $\boldsymbol{E}_{\mathrm{pc}}$ . The charge transfer coefficient $(\alpha)$ and the electron transfer number $(n)$ were calculated to be 0.52 and 1.71 $(\approx2)$ , respectively. Also, the $k_{\mathrm{s}}$ was calculated as $1.02~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ according to Eq. (4), which was much higher than that of the reported electrode of oxygen-containing functional groups modified GCE $(0.51~\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ [50], indicating that the obtained Co/NCNTs/GCE had fast electron transfer capacity toward the reaction of TBHQ.
Meanwhile, the influence of $\mathrm{\pH}$ value on the electrochemical reaction of TBHQ $\mathrm{(0.1\mmol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ ) in $0.1\ \mathrm{mol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ phosphate buffer at $\mathrm{Co/NCNTs/GCE}$ was studied by CV (Fig. 6a) with the pH value ranging from 5.0 to 9.0. The anodic peak current of TBHQ obviously increased while raising $\mathrm{\pH}$ value from 5.0 to 7.0 (Fig. 6b), and then, the peak current started to decrease. The maximum peak current of TBHQ was achieved at neutral solution due to the proton groups of TBHQ which contributed to the reduction the hydroxyl bond energies so as to improve electron transfer through $\mathrm{\Gamma_{O-H\cdotsN}}$ [51], demonstrating that $\mathrm{\pH}$ value of 7.0 was the optimum condition for further experiments. Furthermore, the redox peak potentials shifted to negative values as the $\mathrm{\pH}$ value of the solution increased from 5.0 to 9.0, implying that protons participated in the electrochemical redox reaction of TBHQ. The linear relationship between anodic peak potentials and $\mathrm{\pH}$ was obtained for TBHQ, and the linear regression equation was $E_{\mathrm{{pa}}}=0.3652\mathrm{~-~}0.0604\mathrm{~\pH}$ $R^{2}=0.971$ ). According to Eq. (5):
$$
\frac{\mathrm{d}E_{\mathrm{p}}}{\mathrm{dpH}}=\frac{2.303m R T}{n F}
$$
wherein $m$ and $n$ are the number of the protons and electrons, respectively. According to the slope of the $E_{\mathrm{p}}{\mathrm{-pH}}$ plot, the $m/n$ value was counted to be 1.08, implying that the electrochemical redox of TBHQ at Co/NCNTs/GCE involved equal number of electrons and protons [16]. The above results showed that the redox reaction of TBHQ at Co/NCNTs/GCE should be a two proton and two-electron process [19, 23].
## 3.5 Optimization of Co/NCNTs amount for electrode modification
The amount of Co/NCNTs applied on the electrode surface was optimized by using DPV. The effect of the amount of Co/NCNTs suspension casting onto the GCE surface on the anodic peak current of TBHQ $\mathrm{\langle0.1\mmol{\cdot}L^{-1}},$ ) is shown in Fig. 7. Clearly, as the volume of Co/NCNTs composites increased on the electrode surface, the redox peak currents significantly increased from 2 to ${8\upmu1}$ and then decreased gradually. It might be due to the fact that the less modified electrode cannot adsorb enough TBHQ on the electrode surface, while an over-modified electrode resulted in a thicker film limited mass transport. Therefore, we chose $8~\upmu\mathrm{l}~\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{NCNTs}$ suspension $(1.0~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{ml}^{-1})$ as the optimal loading amount.
## 3.6 Electrochemical detection of TBHQ
DPV technique was performed to quantitatively detect TBHQ due to its high sensitivity. Figure 8a plots the DPV responses of TBHQ to various concentrations of $0.05{-}80.00~\mathrm{\upmumol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ at the Co/NCNTs/GCE. The anodic current of TBHQ gradually increased with the concentration $(C)$ ranging from 0.05 to $2.00\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ and 2 to $80~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ with linear regression equations of $I_{\mathrm{pa}}=$ $1.351C+10.12$ $(R^{2}=0.996)$ and $I_{\mathrm{pa}}=6.616C+0.4676$ $(R^{2}=0.991)$ , respectively (Fig. 8b). The limit of detection (LOD) was calculated to be as low as $10\ \mathrm{nmol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ for TBHQ (signal/noise of $S/N=3)$ ), which was markedly lower than those in kinds of reported sensors (Table 1 [15, 18–21, 25, 51–55]). It was no doubt that the proposed platform in this work possessed comparable or better performance than the others, which held promising application for detection of TBHQ.
The possible interference study for detecting $5~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}{\cdot\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ TBHQ with other compounds, including inorganic ions $\left(500\ \upmu{\mathrm{mol}}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\ \mathrm{Na}^{+}$ , ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ and $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+},$ ),
