organic compounds $(50\ \mathrm{\textmumol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ glucose, ascorbic acid, L-aspartic acid and $5~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ hydroquinone) and other synthetic antioxidants $(5~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ butylated hydroxyanisole (BHA), propyl gallate (PG), and butylated hydroxytoluene (BHT)) was exhibited in Fig. 9. For BHA, the first scan anodic peak was attributed to the oxidation of the hydroxyanisole group to quinine in a two-electron process. In the successive scans, anodic and cathodic peaks of BHA were very similar to the redox peaks of TBHQ, respectively. For analytical purpose, the first scan anodic peaks for BHA and TBHQ can be used for quantitative analysis [18, 24]. The results indicated that different concentrations of the interfering analyses only caused negligible changes (less than $5\%$ ) when the other substances were added, manifesting that the obtained electrode had good selectivity and was suitable for the selective detection of TBHQ.
The repeatability of the Co/NCNTs electrode was also proved in $50~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}{\cdot\mathrm{L}^{-1}}$ TBHQ solutions for six modified electrodes prepared with identical methods. Six electrodes got a comparable current response and the relative standard deviation (RSD) value of $3.7\%$ for TBHQ. The long-time stability of $\mathrm{Co/NCNTs/GCE}$ was tested by storing it at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After 10 days, the current responses of Co/NCNTs/ GCE decreased by less than $10\%$ of the initial response. The results above confirmed that this modified electrode had excellent repeatability and good stability.
## 3.7 Real sample analysis
In order to assess the potentials of Co/NCNTs/GCE, it was applied to detect TBHQ in the soybean oil and sesame oil samples, which were purchased from the local supermarkets. The samples $(5.0\ \mathrm{g})$ were dissolved in alcohol and centrifuged at $3000\ \mathrm{r{min}^{-1}}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ ; then, the extract procedure was repeated three times using ethanol as extract solvent. Typically, $200~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ soybean oil and sesame oil samples were, respectively, diluted to $10~\mathrm{ml}$ phosphate buffer $(0.1\ \mathrm{mol{\cdot}L^{-1}})$ , and then, they were analyzed by DPV. In addition, the standard addition method was applied to achieve recovery value. The results are shown in Table 2. It was found that the recoveries for the oil samples ranged from $98.9\%$ to $102.2\%$ . The obtained results matched well with the HPLC method, demonstrating that $\mathrm{Co}/$ NCNTs/GCE modified electrode was highly suitable for the detection of TBHQ in edible oil samples.
## 4 Conclusion
In summary, Co/NCNTs composites were successfully synthesized via the direct pyrolysis of ZIF-67 and were modified GCE for sensitive and selective detection of TBHQ. The proposed Co/NCNTs/GCE delivered excellent electrocatalytic activity for the electrochemical oxidation of TBHQ with high selectivity, good stability and excellent reproducibility. The exceptional electrochemical activity may be attributed to the chemical composition and the stable structure as well as good conductivity and high surface area of the composites. Moreover, the designed $\mathrm{Co}/$ NCNTs/GCE can be efficiently used for the assay of TBHQ in food samples. The above unique strategy offers a new avenue for fabrication of other MOF-derived materials sensors.
